WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:08.509
So let's talk about some texture optimization pipelines here.

2
00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:13.789
Again, we're in the project settings window, so just go to edit project settings.

3
00:00:13.789 --> 00:00:18.910
And in the rendering tab, we can enable virtual texture support.

4
00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.870
So what does enabling virtual texture support do?

5
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:28.989
So we can convert things to a virtual texture and we can essentially decide how the streaming method is affected.

6
00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:34.909
So in your texture, you can change the sampler type to virtual color.

7
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:42.030
And you'll see in the content browser, you'll have an icon there that says VT,

8
00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.429
just to show you that you've changed this to a virtual texture.

9
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:54.829
And so talking about texture creation and whether we are streaming, not streaming,

10
00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:00.460
virtual, virtually streaming, you'll see the three different boxes that we have here.

11
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:08.700
And you'll see that we've imported the texture, a 1024, at the bottom one it's a 1000 by 1000.

12
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:15.900
But you can essentially see the texture properties by just double clicking the texture in your

13
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:21.819
content browser and you'll see that by changing the method, whether it's streamed or virtually

14
00:01:21.819 --> 00:01:29.500
streamed or not streamed, this has an impact on how this resource is used and the size that

15
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:36.700
it is used at. And you can see that the resource size is massively different depending on how the

16
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:43.900
texture is then streamed. So if you're not streaming any textures, for example, that resource

17
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:50.540
size is going to be much higher than if you're trying to stream it at runtime and then even

18
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:57.090
lower if you virtually stream the texture. So again, this depends on your project requirements

19
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:02.049
and what kind of output you need and the visual fidelity of the textures that you need.

20
00:02:02.849 --> 00:02:07.890
But just thinking about how you're streaming the textures can have a big impact on the resource

21
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:15.389
size of that specific texture. So when we talk about MIP mapping as well, depending on

22
00:02:16.509 --> 00:02:22.189
the size and the use of the texture, if you're MIP mapping the textures, so say we've got a

23
00:02:22.189 --> 00:02:29.229
1496 texture and you're seeing it close up and everything is nice and crisp and your texture

24
00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:36.530
looks great, but then if you want to MIP map it, Unreal will take some affordances. So if you

25
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:44.770
then see a distance, it will optimize the texture so that it will reduce in quality the

26
00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:51.009
further away that you are, for example. So going from 1496 to 512 at a distance,

27
00:02:51.009 --> 00:02:56.770
we're getting a lower fidelity technically out of the texture. However, because it's further away,

28
00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:03.330
it's less perceivable to the eye at a further distance. So MIP mapping can really help

29
00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:09.250
when you're talking about resource size and imagine if we weren't MIP mapping and we had

30
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:15.650
1496 textures and they were just on every asset in the game and we weren't MIP mapping,

31
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:21.650
therefore we weren't trying to optimize the texture and everything was being rendered at its full

32
00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.210
output, then you're going to have some performance issues. And maybe you want that,

33
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:31.409
maybe you want the crispest highest quality and you're running on the highest end of hardware

34
00:03:31.409 --> 00:03:36.849
and you want to keep everything preserved. Again, it's the trade-off and the push and pull between

35
00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:45.729
the desired platform and what this project is for. A real-time game that's on a console,

36
00:03:45.729 --> 00:03:51.490
for example, will have very different requirements than an Archviz project running

37
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:57.650
on a high-end PC, which is a small containing scene, for example. So again, there are trade-offs,

38
00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:03.569
but MIP mapping is something to consider there. And we can see with the different view modes,

39
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:08.689
with MIP mapping turned on, you see we're getting this fall off towards the horizon line,

40
00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:13.490
whereas the MIP mapping when it's turned off, Unreal is still trying to render it and it

41
00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:20.129
starts to be a little bit noisy. It's still maintaining its compression there and it's trying

42
00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:26.610
to render, but you can see the difference more towards the horizon line as you see MIP mapping

43
00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:33.899
turned on between on and off there. And this is just a good way to visualize the texture

44
00:04:34.459 --> 00:04:40.860
resolution and what's going on with the MIP mapping here. So just using this render mode

45
00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:47.579
will help kind of visualize the resources and where they are being spent in your scene.

46
00:04:47.579 --> 00:04:53.819
And Unreal comes with a lot of good texture optimization view modes, which are good to enable

47
00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:59.660
and disable depending on what you're trying to see, but this is just a good visual representation

48
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:01.339
of that.
